Controller ctrl;                       // AUTO: GPU if present, else CPU reference
if (!ctrl.ok()) { /* no usable backend — the only failure is reported here */ }

// rows: n × (EcdsaRecord(129) + block-fk(3)); key_size = 3 (the opaque tail)
std::vector<uint8_t> results(n);
ctrl.verify_ecdsa(rows, n, /*key_size=*/3, results.data());   // results[i] == 1 valid / 0 invalid
ctrl.verify_schnorr(srows, n, /*key_size=*/3, results.data());

// In-place: VALID rows get their key tail zeroed; INVALID rows keep it intact.
std::span<MultisigRow> batch = ...;       // MUTABLE
ctrl.collect_multisig(batch);
for (auto& row : batch)                    // survivors = the rejected set (m|n+group+block-fk intact)
    if (tail_nonzero(row)) rejected.push_back(row);

// multisig record IS the ECDSA record; threshold record IS the Schnorr record.
// m|n+group+block-fk is the opaque key tail (key_size = 6).
ufsecp_lbtc_verify_ecdsa  (ctrl, ms_rows, n, /*key_size=*/6, results); // multisig table
ufsecp_lbtc_verify_schnorr(ctrl, th_rows, n, /*key_size=*/6, results); // threshold table
ufsecp_lbtc_verify_ecdsa  (ctrl, e_rows,  n, /*key_size=*/3, results); // ecdsa table
ufsecp_lbtc_verify_schnorr(ctrl, s_rows,  n, /*key_size=*/3, results); // schnorr table

The current bridge methods already accept an arbitrary row count and internally split the work into hardware-sized chunks.

So from the caller side the format remains unchanged:

    rows = [record | optional correlation key] * n

For example:

    ufsecp_lbtc_verify_ecdsa(ctrl, rows, row_count, key_size, results);

or for collect mode:

    ufsecp_lbtc_verify_ecdsa_collect(ctrl, rows, row_count, key_size);

You do not need to pre-split the batch unless you want to for your own mmap / scheduling reasons. The bridge walks the input in internal chunks and dispatches each chunk to GPU or CPU fallback.

So sending a very large row count is logically fine, including “a billion rows”, assuming:
- the row buffer is addressable and remains valid for the duration of the call;
- `results` has `row_count` bytes for the results API;
- `row_count * stride` fits the platform address space / `size_t`;
- the mmap/page-cache behavior is acceptable for that one long call.

The bridge should not fail merely because the count is larger than the device batch size. Device-sized batching is handled inside the bridge. The main practical limits are memory mapping, addressability, result-buffer size, and how much latency you want in a single call before control returns to the caller.

